#### Problem 3: Authorization Logic Scattered Across Codebase

Permission checks embedded in business logic make policies difficult to audit, update, and test.

**Current State Example:**
```csharp
public async Task<ClaimApprovalResult> ApproveClaimAsync(string claimId, decimal approvedAmount)
{
    var claim = await _dbContext.Claims.FindAsync(claimId);
    var currentUser = _httpContextAccessor.HttpContext.User;

    // Authorization logic mixed with business logic
    var userRole = currentUser.FindFirst("role")?.Value;
    var userState = currentUser.FindFirst("state")?.Value;

    if (userRole != "ClaimsProcessor" && userRole != "ClaimsSupervisor")
    {
        throw new UnauthorizedException("User cannot approve claims");
    }

    if (claim.ServiceState != userState)
    {
        throw new UnauthorizedException("User cannot approve claims from other states");
    }

    if (approvedAmount > 10000 && userRole != "ClaimsSupervisor")
    {
        throw new UnauthorizedException("Only supervisors can approve claims over $10K");
    }

    // Business logic continues...
    claim.Status = ClaimStatus.Approved;
    claim.ApprovedAmount = approvedAmount;
    await _dbContext.SaveChangesAsync();

    return new ClaimApprovalResult { Success = true };
}
```

**Problems:**
- **Cannot Audit Policies**: Security team must review thousands of lines of business logic to find authorization checks
- **Difficult to Update**: Changing authorization rule requires modifying business logic, testing, and deployment
- **Testing Complexity**: Unit tests must mock authorization context, not just business logic
- **Duplicate Logic**: Same permission checks reimplemented across multiple services
- **No Policy Versioning**: Cannot track when authorization rules changed or who approved changes

